    <h2 id="bali-nomads" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">4. Bali Income Tax Rules for Remote Workers & Nomads</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">
      Bali has become a global hub for remote workers, but the intersection of digital nomadism and Indonesian tax law is fraught with gray areas. Many nomads operate under the assumption that if their clients are overseas and their money is paid into a foreign bank account, they owe nothing to Indonesia. This is a dangerous oversimplification.
    </p>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">
      If you are residing in Bali on a standard tourist visa (B211A) or social visa and stay under 183 days, you are a non-resident. Provided you do not earn income from Indonesian sources or conduct commercial activities locally, you generally have no tax reporting obligations in Indonesia.
    </p>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">
      However, the introduction of the E33G Remote Worker Visa changes the landscape. While it legalizes remote work for foreign employers, if you stay for more than 183 days, you cross the threshold into tax residency. Unless you qualify for the Omnibus Law territorial exemption (which requires specific qualifications and approvals), your worldwide income becomes taxable in Indonesia, regardless of where your clients are located. Operating a local business, consulting for Indonesian clients, or managing local real estate (such as sub-leasing a villa) immediately creates a local tax liability.
    </p>

    <h2 id="kitas-npwp" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">5. KITAS / KITAP Visa & NPWP Registration</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">
      If you hold a work permit (Employment KITAS - E23/C312) or an Investor KITAS (E28A/C313/C314), you have firmly established an intent to reside. Consequently, you are legally obligated to register for an NPWP (Nomor Pokok Wajib Pajak), which is Indonesia’s Tax Identification Number.
    </p>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">
      <strong>How to get an NPWP:</strong> Registration can typically be done online via the DJP portal (ereg.pajak.go.id) or in person at the local tax office (KPP) corresponding to your registered address. You will need your passport, KITAS, and a domicile letter. Note that Indonesia is transitioning to using the National Identity Number (NIK) as the primary tax identifier, and foreign residents with a KITAS are also being integrated into this system.
    </p>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Crucial Step for Expats Leaving Indonesia:</strong> When your assignment ends or you decide to leave Bali for good, you cannot simply fly out. You must formally cancel your KITAS (EPO - Exit Permit Only) and apply to deregister your NPWP. Failure to close your NPWP means you remain a tax resident in the eyes of the DJP, and you will continue to accrue penalties for unfiled tax returns.
    </p>

    <h2 id="dta-treaties" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">6. Double Taxation Agreements (DTAs)</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">
      To prevent the same income from being taxed by both your home country and Indonesia, the Indonesian government maintains over 70 Double Taxation Avoidance Agreements (DTAs). These tax treaties are lifesavers for expats and foreign investors.
    </p>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">
      If you are a non-resident earning Indonesian-sourced income (such as dividends, royalties, or interest), the standard 20% PPh 26 withholding tax can often be reduced to 10% or 15% under a DTA. To claim this reduced rate, you cannot simply tell your Indonesian payor that you are a foreign resident. You must provide a valid Certificate of Domicile (SKD - Surat Keterangan Domisili) issued by the tax authority of your home country, completed on the specific DJP DGT form.
    </p>

    <h2 id="compliance" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">7. Deadlines, Compliance, & Penalties</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">
      The Indonesian tax year runs concurrently with the calendar year (January 1 to December 31). As an individual tax resident, your most critical deadline is <strong>March 31st</strong>. By this date, you must file your Annual Individual Income Tax Return (SPT Tahunan). Depending on your income structure, you will file either Form 1770 (for those with business income or freelance work) or Form 1770 S (for employees).
    </p>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Penalties for Non-Compliance:</strong> The DJP does not take kindly to late filing or underpayment. Late filing of an individual tax return incurs a flat penalty of IDR 100,000. However, late payment of taxes incurs a floating monthly interest penalty (based on the Ministry of Finance benchmark rate plus a percentage), which compounds rapidly. In cases of intentional tax evasion, criminal penalties, including imprisonment and massive fines (up to 400% of the unpaid tax), can be levied.
    </p>

    <h2 id="faqs" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-10">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="space-y-6 mt-4">
      <div class="p-5 rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft dark:bg-surface dark:border-overlay-lighter">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Do digital nomads in Bali pay income tax in Indonesia?</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed">Foreigners residing in Bali for more than 183 days within a 12-month period are considered tax residents. Under Job Creation Law rules, qualifying foreign residents pay tax only on Indonesian-sourced income for their first 4 years. If you do not qualify for this exemption, worldwide income may be taxable.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-5 rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft dark:bg-surface dark:border-overlay-lighter">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">How much tax do non-resident foreigners pay in Indonesia?</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed">Non-resident foreigners (staying 182 days or less) pay a flat 20% withholding tax (PPh 26) on gross income sourced in Indonesia without PTKP deductions. This rate may be reduced if a valid Double Taxation Agreement (DTA) is in place.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-5 rounded-lg border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft dark:bg-surface dark:border-overlay-lighter">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary">Do KITAS visa holders need an NPWP tax number in Indonesia?</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed">Yes. Foreigners holding a work or stay permit (KITAS/KITAP) who earn income in Indonesia must register for an NPWP (Tax Identification Number) with the DJP. This is a mandatory requirement for maintaining compliance.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft p-6 dark:border-overlay-light dark:bg-surface">
